{"reference":"1 Kings 19","book":"1 Kings","chapter":19,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions of Meaning","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The phrase translated “a still small voice” is difficult to render. The Hebrew expression has been understood in different ways, including “gentle whisper,” “low murmuring sound,” or “sound of thin silence.” The uncertainty means that the scene should not be reduced to a single precise description of what Elijah heard.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 19:11-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_019_b8e68526965d9d2668c23023"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Elijah believes that he alone remains faithful, but the answer names seven thousand in Israel who have not bowed to Baal. Paul later cites this preserved remnant while answering whether God has rejected Israel, answering negatively and identifying himself as an Israelite. This later use stands against claims that God rejected Jews as a people or transferred their identity to Gentile Christians without remainder.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 19:10-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_019_c6b71f0046d39e394558b456"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why It Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s use of Elijah’s preserved remnant makes this passage important to later discussion of whether God has rejected Israel. His answer is no, and the remnant in Elijah’s day is part of that argument.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 19:10-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_019_16c61682e89653a53756f349"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"In the Surrounding Narrative","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Outside this chapter’s narrated events, the Kurkh Monolith names Ahab as part of a coalition at Qarqar. This independently places an Israelite king in ninth-century regional politics, though it does not confirm every detail or theological claim in 1 Kings.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 16:29-34","1 Kings 17:1-24","1 Kings 18:1-46","1 Kings 20:1-43","1 Kings 21:1-29","1 Kings 22:1-40"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_019_f34e6e13f0b8ac6288cdf263"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}